ASDF OPERATE re-evaluates the in-progress top-level LOAD form (→ SIGSEGV under appease).

STATUS: open.  This is the current blocker for the USING-ASDF leg of
uiop-bocl-porting.issue.  DEFSYSTEM works fully; (asdf:operate 'asdf:load-op
"sys") then mis-behaves.

== Symptom ==
Load a file FOO.lisp (via `bocl -l FOO.lisp`) whose top-level forms are, in order:
  (load "modules/asdf/asdf.lisp")                  ; form 1
  ...initialise source-registry, register immutable...
  (asdf:defsystem "hello" :pathname "/abs/dir/" :components ((:file "pkg") ...))
  (asdf:operate 'asdf:load-op "hello")             ; form N

When form N runs, bocl re-evaluates FORM 1 of the SAME file —
  (load "modules/asdf/asdf.lisp")
— but now with *DEFAULT-PATHNAME-DEFAULTS* changed (to the system's tree, e.g.
/home/.../tmp/), so the relative path no longer resolves:

  == about to operate ==
  ;; Unhandled SIMPLE-ERROR: LOAD: file ".../tmp/modules/asdf/asdf.lisp" does not exist

Under --on-error exit this errors out at the first re-entry and the process exits 1.
Under --on-error appease the re-entry is "recovered" and the WHOLE driver re-runs
(its banner / defsystem / operate print a second time), which re-enters OPERATE,
which re-enters the load, ... → unbounded recursion → SIGSEGV (process dies 139).

So the SIGSEGV is a secondary effect (stack overflow from the re-entry, with bocl's
default signal trap + appease turning the fault into another recovery); the PRIMARY
bug is that OPERATE causes the file's top-level form(s) to be evaluated again.

== What is (probably) happening ==
OPERATE's machinery (or a PERFORM/UPGRADE-ASDF step) appears to re-trigger the
top-level LOAD of the file currently being loaded — i.e. the value of
*LOAD-PATHNAME* / the current load stream — rather than (or in addition to)
loading the system's own component files (.../dir/pkg.lisp, main.lisp).  Candidate
mechanisms to check:
  - bocl LOAD re-reading from the top after a deep nested LOAD (stream position /
    *load-pathname* / *load-truename* not saved & restored across a nested LOAD
    inside OPERATE's PERFORM (load-op -> (load component-file))).
  - OPERATE / UPGRADE-ASDF using *LOAD-PATHNAME* (still = the driver) as a system
    or component source.
  - *DEFAULT-PATHNAME-DEFAULTS* being rebound by OPERATE and not restored, so a
    later/re-run relative LOAD resolves against the wrong directory (the changed
    DPD is directly observed in the error path above).

== Repro (deterministic) ==
  ;; /tmp/drv.lisp
  (load "modules/asdf/asdf.lisp")
  (asdf:initialize-source-registry '(:source-registry :ignore-inherited-configuration))
  (asdf:defsystem "hello" :pathname "/abs/asdf-test/"
    :components ((:file "pkg") (:file "main" :depends-on ("pkg"))))
  (format t "about to operate~%") (finish-output)
  (asdf:operate 'asdf:load-op "hello")
  ;; run: bocl --no-init --batch --on-error exit -l /tmp/drv.lisp -quit
  ;; observe the spurious re-load of drv.lisp's FIRST form during OPERATE.

A minimal asdf-test/ system (pkg.lisp defines a package, main.lisp depends on it)
is enough; the component files themselves are never reached because the re-entry
happens first.

== Next steps ==
1. Instrument bocl LOAD / DO-LOAD to log (push/pop) *LOAD-PATHNAME* and the stream,
   and confirm whether a nested LOAD inside PERFORM (load-op) corrupts the outer
   load's position or *load-pathname* — strong suspect: nested LOAD not saving the
   enclosing load's stream/pathname.
2. Check whether OPERATE rebinds *DEFAULT-PATHNAME-DEFAULTS* without UNWIND-PROTECT
   restoring it.
3. Once the re-entry is gone, re-drive (asdf:operate 'load-op "hello") to reach the
   actual component compile/load (PERFORM load-op on the cl-source-files) — the real
   target of the USING-ASDF milestone.

== Related ==
- uiop-bocl-porting.issue (parent: USING ASDF / OPERATE).
- asdf-source-registry-inherit-scan-slow.issue (separate; avoided here via
  :ignore-inherited-configuration).
- bocl traps signals by default (--no-sigtrap disables); the appease recovery of a
  SIGSEGV compounds this — consider not "appeasing" a fatal memory signal.
